{"qid":"Q1048918","names":["Kingdom of the Suebi"],"title":"Kingdom of the Suebi","endonym":{"native":"Regnum Suevorum","lang":"la"},"capsule":"The Suebi crossed the Rhine in 406 CE and entered Iberia by 409, eventually establishing a kingdom centered on Gallaecia (modern Galicia and northern Portugal) with its capital at Braga (Bracara Augusta). Under Rechiar (r. 448–456) they expanded aggressively into Carthaginensis and Baetica before being crushed by the Visigothic king Theoderic II at the Órbigo River in 456. The Suebi converted to Nicene Christianity under Martin of Braga in the 560s, a rare case of an Arian Germanic people embracing Catholicism before political absorption; the Visigothic king Leovigild conquered the kingdom in 585, ending its independent existence.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The kingdom's core territory in Gallaecia is attested through chronicle sources (Hydatius) and bishopric lists, but its fluctuating frontier zones during periods of expansion into southern Iberia are imprecisely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of the Suebi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_the_Suebi"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Martin of Braga","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_of_Braga"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Rechiar","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rechiar"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of the Suebi","rulers":[{"qid":"Q364200","name":"Hermeric","start":411,"end":438,"image":"O rei suevo Hermerico no Corpus Pelagianum (BNE Mss 1513).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/O%20rei%20suevo%20Herme